Right bay — 大二爷伯 (Tua Di Ya Pek)
Image 4
Plaque: 大二爷伯 (Da Er Ye Bo) — "Tua Di Ya Pek"
Plaque: 大二爷伯 (Dà Èr Yé Bó)Who are 大二爷伯大二爷伯 refers to the paired underworld escort deities widely venerated in Singapore and Southeast Asian Hokkien-Teochew folk religious tradition:
大爷 (Dà Yé) — "Big Uncle" / Seventh Lord
  Canonical name:  謝必安 (Xiè Bì'ān)
  Also known as:   白無常 (Bái Wúcháng)
                   White Impermanence
  Appearance:      Very tall, pale/white face,
                   long tongue protruding,
                   white robes, tall black hat
                   (sometimes inscribed 一見大吉)

二爷 (Èr Yé) — "Second Uncle" / Eighth Lord
  Canonical name:  范無救 (Fàn Wú Jiù)
  Also known as:   黑無常 (Hēi Wúcháng)
                   Black Impermanence
  Appearance:      Shorter, dark/black face,
                   black robes, black hat
  
Together:          七爷八爷 (Seventh and Eighth Lords)
                   黑白無常 (Black and White Impermanence)
                   大二爷伯 (Big Uncle and Second Uncle)
Their primary function is as escorts of the dead — the two officials who arrive at the moment of death to conduct the soul from the living world to the underworld realm. Unlike the Ten Kings of Hell who judge souls, or Dizang who intercedes for them, the Big and Second Uncles are the transitional figures who physically accompany the soul across the boundary between life and death.
They are also closely associated with the Hungry Ghost Festival (中元節), during which they are said to be on active duty supervising the release of souls from the underworld. Their presence in this hall is not coincidental.
Ceiling — Image 5
The caisson ceiling (藻井, Zǎo Jǐng) visible in Image 5 is the most architecturally significant element in the Buddhist section.ARCHITECTURAL DESCRIPTION:
  Multi-layered concentric circular caisson ceiling:
  - Outermost ring: elaborate red-and-gold carved decoration
    with cloud/dragon/phoenix relief motifs
  - Second ring: dark lacquered timber with LED lighting
  - Third ring: carved decorative band
  - Fourth ring: geometric pattern with lotus/auspicious motifs
  - Innermost circle: ornate center medallion with carved figure
    or auspicious creature at the very apex
  - Color palette: red lacquer + dark wood + gold accents
  - Modern LED strip lighting integrated into the concentric rings

  The caisson ceiling is one of the most elaborate in
  this corpus and rivals the architectural sophistication
  of the monastery halls analyzed at KMSPKS and LSSL.
Iconographic significance of the caisson ceiling:
The caisson ceiling in Chinese architecture is derived from the 藻井 tradition — literally "algae well" — a term referring to the circular watery motifs used to protect wooden structures against fire through sympathetic magic. In temple architecture, the caisson ceiling serves several functions:
1. Spatial axis marker
   The ceiling's circular center designates the point
   below as the most sacred location in the hall.
   Its positioning above the Dizang altar designates
   Dizang's position as the spatial-cosmological axis
   of the entire section.

2. Vertical cosmological connector
   The caisson ceiling creates an architectural link
   between the terrestrial plane (altar level) and
   the celestial realm above. Given that Dizang's
   vow explicitly spans the vertical axis of Chinese
   cosmology — from the lowest hells to enlightenment
   — the ceiling architecturally enacts this vertical
   soteriological span.

3. Cosmological statement
   The concentric circles encode the Buddhist cosmological
   model: the innermost center (Dizang / Buddha-realm) is
   surrounded by progressively larger circles representing
   the rings of the cosmos outward from the Buddhist axis.

